  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 329 AM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe aforementioned weak frontal boundary is forecast to completely \ndissipate by Tuesday, while the subtropical Atlantic high gradually \nstrengthens and builds eastward towards the peninsula. This will \nusher in a return to more consistent easterly flow across the \nregion, along with a corresponding increase in tropical moisture. \nDew points and precipitable water values should return seasonable \nlevels by mid-week. \n\nA few interesting developments are also expected to take place aloft \nduring this time period. The base of an extensive upper-level trough \nis forecast to remain draped across Florida, while a shortwave \ndisturbance looks likely to slide southward and near South Florida \nby Wednesday. The combination of this disturbance and the influx of \nmoisture promote wetter and more unsettled conditions, with shower \nand thunderstorm chances potentially returning to the 60-80% range \nTuesday-Wednesday. \n\nBy Thursday, ridging looks likely to build aloft over the Gulf, \nwhile the surface pattern remains generally unchanged. This should \nfavor a return to more typical mesoscale-driven convective activity \neach day.",
